# A case where guff deliberately writes what golangci-lint does not.
#
# Hand-written. Nothing in regen.sh creates a file here: the `pending` recorder
# refuses this direction outright, because writing bytes into a file upstream
# leaves alone is how `omitempty` became `omitzero` under a green harness
# (COMPAT-HARDENING 続き 38). The only way past that refusal is for a person to
# write down why, which is what this file is.
#
# why: golangci-lint's revive `--fix` is inert in almost every real repository,
# and by accident rather than by design. pkg/golinters/revive/revive.go looks the
# file up with `Fset.File(token.Pos(failure.Position.Start.Offset))` — a byte
# offset handed to a lookup that wants a FileSet-wide position — and then drops
# the fix unless the file it found is the failure's own. The comment there says
# the guard skips cgo files; what it does is drop every fix outside whichever
# file happens to sit at the start of the FileSet. Measured with golangci-lint
# 2.12.2: one package with a.go and b.go fixes a.go and leaves b.go alone, and a
# module with two packages fixes nothing at all. Which findings keep their fix
# therefore depends on FileSet layout that neither tool guarantees, so it cannot
# be reproduced — the same computation on guff's FileSet suppressed the nine
# revive cases that do match, because guff loads dependency files first.
#
# guff writes the fix revive itself prescribes. It compiles, the findings are
# identical either way (golden is 193/193), and the only cost of the divergence
# is that `guff --fix` improves code `golangci-lint --fix` silently skips.
#
# Fails if guff's bytes move, and fails if upstream ever starts writing here —
# at which point the reason above stops describing reality and someone has to
# decide again.
--- a/bad/revivetest/bad.go
+++ b/bad/revivetest/bad.go
@@ -73,7 +73,7 @@
 var zeroVar int = 0
 
 func badErrorsNew() error {
-	return errors.New(fmt.Sprintf("x %d", 1))
+	return fmt.Errorf("x %d", 1)
 }
 
 type unexported struct{}
